detail.abstract:
Background: The integration of entropy metrics from information theory into orthodontic research represents a paradigm shift toward computational diagnostics and objective treatment assessment. Despite growing applications across imaging analysis, signal processing, and biomechanics, the literature remains fragmented across clinical, engineering, and computational domains without systematic synthesis or methodological standards.&nbsp;
<div>
 Objective: This methodological paper presents and justifies the comprehensive search strategy developed for PROSPERO-registered systematic review CRD420251272662, "Applications of Entropy in Orthodontic Research: A Systematic Review and Conceptual Framework." We det ail an innovative interdisciplinary search methodology designed to overcome domain fragmentation and propose a novel appraisal framework for entropy-specific reporting quality.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Methods: We designed a multi-database search strategy employing biomedical databases (PubMed/MEDLINE, Embase, CENTRAL), multidisciplinary citation indexing (Scopus), and specialized engineering repositories (IEEE Xplore). Supplementary searches included grey literature databases (ProQuest Dissertations &amp; Theses Global, medRxiv, arXiv), citation tracking, and hand-searching. A dual-independent screening process and custom Entropy Application Reporting Quality (EARQ) checklist were developed to ensure rigor. The strategy was validated through pilot testing and will be executed across all platforms simultaneously.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results: The search strategy demonstrates high sensitivity through vocabulary mapping across domains. The PubMed/MEDLINE strategy retrieves comprehensive results when executed as: #1 [entropy terms] AND #2 [orthodontic terms] = #3. Parallel adaptations for other d atabases maintain conceptual consistency while respecting platform-specific syntax. The methodology explicitly addresses the challenge of interdisciplinary retrieval in dental research synthesis. Conclusion: This protocol provides a replicable model for systematic reviews bridging clinical dentistry and computational sciences. By transparently documenting an interdisciplinary search methodology and introducing domain-specific appraisal tools, it advances the methodology of evidence synthesis in technologically evolving dental specialties. The resulting synthesis aims to establish the first conceptual framework for entropy applications in orthodontics, guiding future research toward clinical translation.
</div>
